<h2>The relationship and the complaint</h2>
<p>The petitioner and the complainant were in a relationship from 2022 until May 2026 and lived together for more than four years. The complaint alleged that the petitioner gave the complainant a medicine described as a painkiller after which she miscarried, that a pregnancy was terminated at a Ghaziabad hospital on 31 March 2023 against her wishes, and that when she asked him to marry her he refused — after which the FIR followed. The petitioner&rsquo;s answer was that the relationship was entirely consensual, that it broke down over temperamental differences, that he then returned to his native place in Bihar, that he has no antecedents, and that he had joined the investigation under the interim protection granted on 16 October 2026 — a fact the Additional Public Prosecutor confirmed, while noting that a potency test was pending and the source of the medicine remained to be verified.</p>

<h2>The legal framework the Court applied</h2>
<p>The Court anchored its analysis in the Supreme Court&rsquo;s recent decisions in <em>Samadhan v. State of Maharashtra</em> (2025) and <em>Prashant v. State of NCT of Delhi</em> (2025), which hold that a relationship that ends without marriage is not automatically criminal, and that consent given during a long consensual association cannot be retrospectively converted into rape merely because marriage did not follow. Against that standard, the Court found that &ldquo;it is prima facie established that the petitioner and the complainant lived together for more than four years&rdquo; — a duration consistent with a physical relationship not founded on any promise of marriage. Whether a false promise was in fact made, it held, is a question for trial.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>The contemporaneous record mattered:</strong> the complainant&rsquo;s own letter to the SHO dated 5 June 2026 — before the FIR — described a fight with the petitioner and admitted that the pregnancy arose from their relationship, with no mention of any false promise to marry. As for the miscarriage allegation, the Court observed that &ldquo;the administration of any medicine to the complainant... form the subject matter of investigation and trial as well.&rdquo;</p>
</div>

<h2>The conditions of protection</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li>In the event of arrest, release on a personal bond of Rs 25,000 with one surety of the like amount, to the satisfaction of the IO/SHO.</li>
  <li>Reporting to the investigating officer whenever required, and undergoing any tests sought.</li>
  <li>No contact with the complainant or her family.</li>
  <li>No tampering with evidence; no inducement to or threats against witnesses.</li>
  <li>Mobile phone kept operational, with number changes notified; residential address disclosed and changes intimated; no fresh offence.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>Where this fits in the developing law</h2>
<p>False-promise-of-marriage prosecutions sit at a difficult intersection: genuine deception that vitiates consent is a serious offence, while the criminalisation of every failed relationship is an abuse of the process. Courts have increasingly drawn the line by asking whether the promise was false at its inception and whether it was the operative cause of consent. Long cohabitation, continued association after alleged incidents, and contemporaneous communications that make no mention of a promise all point away from vitiated consent. This decision applies that framework at the anticipatory bail stage: where the prima facie picture is of a consensual adult relationship that soured, custodial interrogation is not the default, especially for an accused who has already joined the investigation. The order decides protection from arrest, not the trial — the allegations about the medicine and the termination remain live issues for the investigation — but it illustrates how the pre-arrest balance is struck in such cases.</p>
<p>One drafting note for practitioners: although the FIR is of 2026, the order records the offences under Sections 376 and 313 IPC, and this article states them as the order does.</p>
